# Rust project-builder API

This directory owns the public Rust project-builder tutorial and the accepted
SDK contract for building Moth project tools in Rust.

Read `creating-a-builder.mtf` first. The focused references then cover Rust host
packages, builder capabilities, compiled assemblies and runtime integration.

## Crate ownership

The public API is split by responsibility:

- `moth-builder` is the high-level project-builder SDK. It depends on the
  canonical `moth` compiler/build library and provides the builder pattern,
  compiled-project views, output services and canonical project-tool driver.
- `moth-host` owns compiler-independent host-package descriptions, runtime
  adapters, host environments, opaque handle tables and artefact/runtime
  contract validation. A deployed Rust application can depend on it without
  linking the compiler.
- `moth-host-macros` implements the Rust attributes and binding macro. It is an
  implementation crate re-exported by `moth-host`, not a second user-facing API.
- `moth` remains the one language/compiler implementation. `moth-builder`
  composes its services rather than reinterpreting Moth semantics.

The crates are modular to keep dependencies and runtime deployments small. They
are deliberately not competing implementations of Moth.

## Reading routes

- Builder lifecycle, definition, driver and low-level embedding:
  `creating-a-builder.mtf`
- Source packages, Rust host functions, supported signatures, host environments
  and opaque resources: `host-packages.mtf`
- Configuration, directives, root purposes, source adapters, import providers
  and target implementations: `capabilities-and-directives.mtf`
- Compiled-project views, template plans, entry errors, lowering, resources,
  outputs and runtime contracts: `assemblies-and-runtime.mtf`

## Shared authorities

`docs/compiler-design-overview.md` owns semantic compilation, canonical types,
result slots, validated compiler handoffs and normal-root execution.

`docs/build-system-design.md` owns Stage 0, builder selection, configuration,
package topology, entry/link planning, lifecycle inputs and output ownership.

Exact directive grammar belongs to `docs/src/docs/directives/directives.mtf`.
Language, package and memory references remain authoritative for source
semantics. The SDK supplies constrained registration and consumer services. It
does not add another parser, type system, borrow model or backend.

## Rules to keep in mind

A `BuilderDefinition` is immutable after validation. Per-build caches and
resolution state belong to the compiler/build session rather than the
definition.

Builder-facing project views never expose donor-local HIR indexes, mutable
`TypeEnvironment`s, string/path interners or analysis side tables. The SDK asks
the compiler for validated operations such as lowering an assembly.

A Rust host annotation generates structured metadata and a checked adapter. It
does not generate Moth source, scan the crate for registrations or expose a
network endpoint. Every package, namespace and binding remains explicitly
registered.

A Rust host package describes one Moth semantic surface. Physical target
implementations may differ, but parameter, result, error and access semantics
do not.

Opaque host resources are runtime-scoped typed handles. They compare by handle
identity with `is` and `is not`, but gain no ordering, arithmetic, map-key or
other language operations automatically.

Normal-root startup retains its success slots and built-in `Error!` channel.
Runtime results are published only after a successful invocation. A typed Moth
error, host fault and compiler failure remain distinct outcomes.

Templates remain ordinary strings and structural resource pieces retain their
origin until a builder supplies a valid output context.
